American actor '''[[Mr. T]]''' has headlined in a number of '''[[comic books]]''' both under his own stage name and in conjunction with his role on the TV series ''[[The A-Team]]''.

==Comics==
===Mr. T and the T-Force===<!-- This section is linked from [[Mr. T and the T-Force]] -->
''Mr. T and the T-Force'' was released in [[1993 in comics|1993]] by [[NOW Comics]]. It was written by [[Pete Stone]] with art by [[Neal Adams]] and then various creative teams following their run.

The series lasted 10 consecutive issues, not including a 1994 annual and various editions of #1, before closing when [[NOW Comics]] went out of business.<ref>{{Cite web | title=Values for Mr. T And The T-Force {{!}} ComicsPriceGuide.com {{!}} Free Comic Book Price Guide | url=https://comicspriceguide.com/titles/mr-t-and-the-t-force/ulyx | access-date=2025-07-17 | website=comicspriceguide.com}}</ref><ref>{{cite web | url=https://gocollect.com/comics/series/mr-t-and-the-t-force | title=Mr. T and the T-Force Comics Values - GoCollect }}</ref> This figure may be incorrect however, with the eleventh issue possibly being the final issue produced.

Many, if not all, issues of ''Mr. T and the T-Force'' included Mr. T trading cards.

===Mr. T===
Another comic version, ''Mr. T'', appeared in [[2005 in comics|2005]] from short-lived British comic publisher APComics. It was pitched to the publisher by writer [[Chris Bunting]] who was joined with pencils by Neil Edwards, inks by [[Randy Emberlin]] and with colours by Don Mackinnon. Mr. T was also listed as a Creative Supervisor on the series and did a round of interviews as publicity for the series launch.<ref name=":0">[https://www.imdb.com/name/nm0001558/publicity Mr. T - Publicity<!-- Bot generated title -->]</ref>

The series got a lot of press interest,<ref>[https://writinguk.blogspot.com/2005/06/t-up.html]{{dead link|date=August 2013}}</ref> announcing itself to be one of the biggest licensing deals in British comic's history.  Ultimately, it had a very short life, most probably due to the closure of APComics. It featured as a six-page preview of APComics upcoming comics<ref>[http://www.silverbulletcomicbooks.com/news/111070737923326.htm MR.T #1 Incentive from APComics: News Bulletins - Comics Bulletin<!-- Bot generated title -->] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20061021034037/http://www.silverbulletcomicbooks.com/news/111070737923326.htm |date=2006-10-21}}</ref><ref>{{usurped|1={{usurped|1=[https://archive.today/20070702164815/http://www.popcultureshock.com/reviews.php?id=4542 APComics 2005 Preview » PopCultureShock<!-- Bot generated title -->]}}}}</ref> and then two issues were released. Apparently the second issue, released several months after the first, was released out of license by APComics and many copies were ordered to be immediately withdrawn.

Richard Emms, owner of APComics, 'left' for [[Markosia]] and with him went a number of titles. However, ''Mr. T'' was not one of those and there were a variety of complaints including of non-payment emerging from creatives working on ''Mr. T''. That appears to have been settled by APComics.<ref>[http://www.comicbookresources.com/columns/index.cgi?column=litg&article=2230 Comic Book Resources - Comic Book News, Reviews and Commentary - Updated Daily!<!-- Bot generated title -->] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060614140847/http://www.comicbookresources.com/columns/index.cgi?column=litg&article=2230 |date=2006-06-14 }}, [[Comic Book Resources]]</ref><ref>[http://www.newsarama.com/forums/showthread.php?threadid=40794 Apc Boss Richard Emms Quits - Joins Markosia]{{dead link|date=July 2025|bot=medic}}{{cbignore|bot=medic}}, [[Newsarama]]</ref> In 2006 new cover art from [[Jim Steranko]] appeared as a US publisher had temporary permission to use the license for the third and final issue in the comic book series.<ref>[https://writinguk.blogspot.com/2006/03/hangin-with-jim-steranko-and-mrt.html]{{dead link|date=August 2013}}</ref> In February [[2008 in comics|2008]] publisher Mohawk Media released an all-new ''Mr. T'' [[graphic novel]]. Entitled ''Mr. T: Limited Advance Edition Graphic Novel'' it was limited to 4,000 copies.<ref>[https://web.archive.org/web/20080225150755/http://www.comicbookresources.com/news/newsitem.cgi?id=13073 Mr. T Celebrates His New Graphic Novel With An Advance Limited Edition] (press release), [[Comic Book Resources]], February 21, 2008</ref> The regular worldwide edition of the ''Mr. T'' [[graphic novel]] was released in December 2008.<ref name="Mr. T graphic novel final release">{{cite web | url=http://www.mohawkmedia.co.uk/cgi-bin/news_details.pl?&id=23 | title=Mr. T: A real black super hero | publisher=Mohawk Media | date=December 15, 2008 | accessdate=August 15, 2013}}</ref> Later that same month, Mohawk Media announced a line of merchandise and gift products based on the ''Mr. T'' [[graphic novel]].<ref>[http://www.mohawkmedia.co.uk/cgi-bin/news_details.pl?&id=24 Mr. T teams up with Darth Vader, Barbie and Mickey Mouse!] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20081225005054/http://www.mohawkmedia.co.uk/cgi-bin/news_details.pl?&id=24 |date=2008-12-25}} (press release), Mohawk Media, December 19, 2008</ref>

===A-Team comics===
{{main| The A-Team (comics)}}
Mr. T appeared, as B. A. Baracus, in ''The A-Team'' comic adaptation that appeared in ''[[Look-In]]'' for throughout the 1980s. An ''A-Team'' strip also ran in ''[[TV Comic]]'' sold in UK. Length of run is unknown.

From {{start date|1983}} to {{end date|1984}}, [[Marvel Comics]] released an ''A-Team'' title which ran for 3 issues.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.comics-db.com/Marvel_Comics/A/The_A-Team/ |title=The A-Team Comic Book Resource |publisher=Comics-db.com |accessdate=2013-08-16 |url-status=usurped |archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20070927175508/http://www.comics-db.com/Marvel_Comics/A/The_A-Team/ |archivedate=2007-09-27}}</ref> Due to the huge popularity of both comics and ''The A-Team'' show at that time, information which lists this as a 3-issue 'mini-series' is probably accurate. The Marvel Comics series was sold individually then released in "packs": one pack contained the first 2 issues, the next all 3 issues. Each pack was a sealed transparent bag featuring Marvel's logo and a picture of Spider-Man. Marvel released the series in a [[trade paperback (comics)|trade paperback]] called ''The A-Team Storybook''. ({{ISBN|0517553856}}).

The Marvel US comics were also reprinted by [[Marvel UK]] in a variety of formats including hardback annual book and ''The A-Team Summer Special 1985'' and ''The A-Team Summer Special #2'' (1986). The final Marvel UK hardback ''A-Team'' annual was released in 1991, several years after the cancellation of ''The A-Team'' TV series, showing its continued popularity in the UK despite this.

In 2010, [[IDW Publishing]] issued several comic books tying in with [[The A-Team (film)|''The A-Team'' film]], but neither of these feature Mr. T, but rather actor [[Rampage Jackson|Quinton Jackson]], who took over the role of B. A. Baracus for the film.

